{
  "title": "Implement and calibrate the directional skew-pair statistic for held-out evaluation",
  "statement": "In an exploratory public-training comparison, one-to-one substitution leaves the measured pair statistic and distinct-type share exactly unchanged in every one of twelve 9,000-token samples. An independently randomized three-homophone-per-character encoding reduces skewed pair-type counts sharply and increases distinct-type share. This does not establish a manuscript result or model a particular historical cipher: the toy homophonic code permits three equiprobable signs for every character, and the scorer currently has no skew-pair statistic. A 3,000-token pilot was inspected before choosing the reported 9,000-token windows, so the results are exploratory.",
  "program": "J",
  "on_path": true,
  "hard": false,
  "check": "Implement a reviewed scorer primitive for the paper’s directional pair statistic and its unique-word measure. Resolve the paper’s ambiguous pair denominator, stop-word percentage and unique-word definition before fixing a specification. Calibrate fixed window sizes, tokenization, sample-matched historical texts and cipher controls, and fix thresholds before measuring hidden manuscript pages. Verify monoalphabetic invariance, power against planted asymmetric sequences, multiple seeded homophonic and natural-language controls, and independent transliterations. The current local pilot does not satisfy the hidden-page part of K-043-open-1.",
  "local_method": {
    "source": "https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3313/paper8.pdf",
    "samples": "Three consecutive 9,000-word windows from each public training text: Latin, Italian, English, medieval Czech and certain paragraph tokens of ZL manuscript training pages. Windows are not independent corpora.",
    "filter": "Remove the single most common token separately from each sample and treatment. This explicit pilot policy is not asserted to reproduce the paper’s percentage-based stopword rule.",
    "statistic": "After filtering, count ordered unequal-token pairs at forward distances one through three. Each unordered pair type counts once in the denominator; the numerator counts those with absolute forward-minus-reverse frequency greater than five. Unique ratio is distinct token types divided by retained tokens, not necessarily the paper’s intended meaning.",
    "transforms": "One-to-one fixed substitution and independent uniform choice among three disjoint codes per plaintext character, seeds 1404–1406. Five independently shuffled token-order controls per sample/treatment. Code labels are fixed-width, so concatenation remains unambiguous.",
    "limitations": "Not a full replication: no historical cipher implementation, text-boundary control, frequency-threshold sensitivity, independent confirmation, or hidden-page evaluation; random 3,000-word pilot observed first. Monoalphabetic invariance is expected by construction.",
